{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs. A new law will take effect on Sunday, making it a crime to possess small amounts of hard drugs. The new law gives people caught with illicit substances the option to be charged or undergo treatment. Treatment includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a program to avoid fines.\n\nUnder the new law, possession of small amounts of hard drugs will be a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail. The law also aims to make it easier for police to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public areas. \n\nThe law encourages counties to develop alternative treatment programs to divert people from the justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. However, opponents argue that this may create a confusing and inequitable system of policies.\n\nPortland police chief Bob Day stated that officers will call a deflection dispatch line if a person meets the requirements for deflection. They will then connect the person to a mobile behavioral health outreach team that will have 30 minutes to make contact. If the team cannot make contact within 30 minutes, the person will be taken to jail.\n\n*** \n\nP.P.S. As the new law takes effect in Oregon, it's likely that many people in the country will be talking about the issue. Some may even be thinking about how it will affect their shopping habits or whether they will be able to eat at their favorite restaurants if they're caught with small amounts of illicit substances. But for now, the focus is on the new law and its potential impact on the age-old problem of drug addiction. Just like a person trying to learn a new language, it will take time and practice to understand t